  "query_preview": "period of clear skies and subsequent radiative cooling \novernight. Temperatures have dropped into the low-mid 30s as of 3 \nAM, with another few hours of cooling expected to bring lows into \nthe upper 20s in some spots. The decoupled boundary layer sits \nbeneath ongoing WAA where h8 temperatures peak at 7-8 C, meaning as \ndiurnal mixing erodes the inversion, temperatures climb into the mid \n50s. Veering vertical wind profiles also show strengthening low \nlevel winds throughout the day, which the growing boundary layer \nwill tap into to produce breezy WSW winds gusting to 30 mph during \nthe daylight hours. \n\nLooking at GOES-19 satellite imagery, a band of mid-high cloud is \naccelerating eastward into Lower Michigan on the nose of a 90-95 \nknot upper level jet (noted as the dry slot in mid level WV \nimagery). This moisture corridor represents the lead edge of height \nfalls associated with the mature stacked low over western Ontario, \nwhich sends a weak surface front across SE Michigan by early this \nafternoon. While the frontal passage itself will be dry, subsequent \ncold advection will add to the moisture pool by establishing \nmoisture flux off of the lake aggregate. Hi-res models show a \nconvergence signal over the Saginaw Valley/Thumb which could lead to \nshower activity across these portions of the forecast area, so long \nas lake effect bands do not set up entirely offshore. Elsewhere, \nstratus expands south for mostly cloudy skies overnight. \n\nCloud cover and lake induced showers linger into Monday morning \nbefore moisture is scoured out by the brief influence of high \npressure. Surface pressure during the first half of the week \nfluctuates between 1010-1020mb in a progressive, low amplitude \npressure pattern. The weak nature of the waves has little impact on \ntemperature prospects, with Tuesday actually being the warmest day \nof the period (highs in the low 60s) despite a surface trough \npassing through earlier in the day. Reliance on Pacific moisture \njust does not provide enough depth for any meaningful rain chances \nover land through Wednesday. \n\nA more pronounced pattern shift arrives late in the week as a much \nstronger low slides across the Ohio Valley, followed by colder \nCanadian air crashing in from the north. This could introduce our \nfirst shot at mixed precipitation with the arrival of the coldest \nair of the season.",
